{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/64194-blasphemy-the-ninth","id":64194,"title":"Blasphemy: the Ninth","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-02-03T12:13:13-08:00","id":64194,"title":"Blasphemy: the Ninth","content":"<span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">[A story merging the hooks in Blasphemies and the Rage]<br /> <br /> This story is true: Once there was nine, now there are ten. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">The Watcher Wolf, </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Udzhalla-Ur</span></i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">, shared many things in common with her sisters, Red Wolf and Death Wolf: she was curious, she wanted to understand. In the days of the Firstborn, she was the first to spot humans or spirits that had crossed worlds to meddle with the balance, and told Father Wolf. She never lost sight of prey, and kept watch for spirits which would attack </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Urfarah</span></i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">. Yet, she was odd, disliked by her siblings. She never gave chase, never hunted, she only watched. Enemies and prey both could scurry over her nose, and </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Udzhalla</span></i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> just tilted her head. She wanted something no other wolf - no other spirit - wanted: to know what else there was, beyond Pangaea.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">It was Watcher Wolf that sought the paths which led to the Celestials; they say she met Luna first, and the goddess took her road to court Father Wolf. It was Watcher Wolf that sought the roads that the dead travel; they say she hunted the prey who would not stay dead. It was Watcher Wolf that sought the way into dreams. They say she found Order, Chaos, and Passion, and they followed her footsteps back to humanity.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Watcher Wolf tread in the places she did not belong, and in doing so, introduced things to the world which did not belong here; she changed Pangaea. They say that Watcher Wolf saw the plot to kill Father Wolf, but she did not tell him. Watcher Wolf is the Firstborn of Unintended Consequences. Watcher Wolf is cursed.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">She lost something in those travels. Maybe it was gradual, leaving a little bit of herself wherever she went. Maybe she sacrificed herself, a little bit, in order to </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i><span style=\"font-size:15px\">create</span></i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> the forbidden paths she tread. Maybe in the dark realms, something found her, and tore her apart in one bite. Maybe her betrayals drove her to die from grief. Maybe. But this is true: Watcher Wolf is a spirit without a soul.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Now two wolves walk. A healthy gray wolf circles the world, watching without eyes - only black, glossy orbs as hungry as they are empty; a wolf that never reacts, never shows interest in its surroundings - but it sees, clear through the world, to its only prey: </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Zi\u2019ir</span></i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">, or rather, what they have lost. Also walking among us, unseen and unheard, are the wisps of paws belonging to the restless shadow of a wolf. Clear, white, cold, and full of regret is wolf seeking to amend the consequences of its curiosity, but unable to touch the world.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">We do not speak of the Ninth Firstborn, but we should. </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Udzhalla-Ur </span></i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">did things she should not have - she abandoned the natural order - and became something broken and dangerous as a consequence. It is because of her that we have Tribes and Oaths; because of her, we strive for balance within ourselves and in the world. To abandon what it means to be </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Uratha</span></i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\"> is to be lost to oneself, a broken soul or a homeless wanderer, who has no effect on The People.<br /> <br /> We do not speak of <i>Udzhalla-Ur</i>, but we should. Because she watches still, there are Ten Tribes. Two are guided silently by a Soulless Wolf and its Ghost. Two are broken, and they unravel what we are meant to be.</span></span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-04T12:20:29-08:00","id":65060,"title":"","content":"Ok i'll bite. So is she like a sister to the soulless wolf we see from blasphemies? Representative of those broken souled wolves?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> WoD-Dark Eras!! (Backed for Viking Age Werewolf)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1939-lyrics-of-war","name":"Lyrics Of War","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1939_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1769"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-04T12:59:17-08:00","id":65080,"title":"","content":"Not precisely. The hooks I used were Soulless Wolf, and the bits about Ghost Wolves from <i>the Rage</i> - namely that ghost wolves all take on a similar appearance (like a ghost) as their Primal Urge increases, suggesting that some unifying but silent force is watching out for them, and the suggestion of ghost wolves finding a tribal totem; Watcher Wolf is an example of a Firstborn a Storyteller might add to the setting. And Soulless wolf was described as a guardian totem, silently watching over the Bale Hounds to keep them from detection, so . . .<br /> <br /> In this version of the myth, Watcher Wolf was a Firstborn. Something was ripped out of her, and she became Soulless Wolf and a kind of phantom spirit: one is &quot;real&quot; but missing the vital part that makes it a true spirit, and is totem for twisted sin-possessed werewolves; the other has a <i>purpose</i> like the other Firstborn, which should have been expressed in a Tribe, but is incapable of influencing either world beyond a vague, subconscious nudge on Ghost Wolves. <br /> Plus some added spin about how werewolves should choose a tribe and keep up their harmony (confusing Zi'ir with Bale Hounds, and adding a little extra creep factor Soulless Wolf - he hunts the bits of you that are lost as Harmony degrades, trying to fill the soul he lost).<br /> <br /> It's nothing much, just a little myth snippet that came to mind when I was reading those sections. I'm kind of collecting/making Blasphemous myths at the moment, as its kind of theme in the chronicle I'm running. I jotted it down as it came to mind, and threw it up on the forums for not particular reason.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-05T10:25:10-08:00","id":65798,"title":"","content":"In my home game, and it may not make sense to others, but here's what happened.<br /> Father Wolf died. Became the first geister. Attached to what-would-become-Soulless Wolf, tried to tell the others how to do things, rather than to just do what he did.<br /> They turned on him, calling him an abomination, thinking he profaned Father Wolf's spirit by sealing it in himself, thinking he wanted the status for his own, held him down, Death Wolf did a rite to rip Father Wolf from him, creating a Soulless Wolf. Justification is, well, none of us play Geist, but from what little I've looked in the book for it I saw that when a Sin-Eater loses their geister, by killing it or whatever, they become soulless. In addition, they're Sin-Eaters, and Soulless Wolf is also a Sin-Eater (from Blasphemies, though he does it via taking other's harmony sins into himself). I thought it was a nice fit, if a little forced.<br /> Anyways, his motive is pretty much about the same as from the book, Destroying the Sanctity of Balance, etc.<br /> The idea being mostly that Father Wolf was not doing a job, he was doing what he truly wanted, his true path. His children just blindly mimicking him was about as perverted as he could imagine.<br /> <br /> Of course, it romanticizes them a bit but they are not good guys or anything, I just thought the pieces fit up too well and Soulless Wolf's history was too empty.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-05T13:21:08-08:00","id":65912,"title":"","content":"I like it. That's a pretty world view -shaking story, and leaves a lot of interesting avenues open.<br /> It feels a little forced and a little off from how Geist works, but it fits for your chronicle, so that's no big deal.<br /> <br /> I dislike saying &quot;this is what happened&quot; and prefer to present several contradictory myths with a little but of truth and evidence in each of them, but that's my style - and what my chronicle's doing. Besides, having that actually be the case gives you more solid plot opportunity. I mean, Father Wolf as an unfettered (or worse, a newly Bound) geist is a terrifying and awesome story hook.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}